{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-318223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the three line segments for $x\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+3+7 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-318223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/7aff74d99be667ea7b51bfcfaee2c0e701e3a553656aa931ba3f1f1ba0115ffb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [0,1]$ and the piecewise linear for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-51443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-343468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/48fc0e80cf6e2ff5a83a659a53afc05ce8b7343edd4377b8cb935d1abb480514.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-343468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ the line segment for $x\\in [3,5],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [5,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =4+2+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-343468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-343468" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-343468" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/3e511986dc068a34c72397bed12b81b94dd2150cfba24efe8245318b42f23fdd.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$, the line segment for $x\\in [0,2]$ and the two line segments for $x\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-5.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{9}{2}-5-7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15"] ["math" "-\\frac{15}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{29}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{37}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{37}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{29}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/f8810b09e23c88d5833b43804cf62f08ce91dfa426a0d9363a579ecff2bca715.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [0,2]$, the line segment for $x\\in [2,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,5].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =5+2+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "19"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-17914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{19}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/95e601203c55e93922554e4eb56a9742f4a84466ac387e3358484baaf9287e08.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-318225" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-318225" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+2\\pi"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/64f3e5d99f302a567d6299ee5036a1efa728bae0e21f5905d3698241e1abdfa1.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(-2,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2]$, the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [-2,0]$, the line segment for $x\\in [0,1]$, and the line segment for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={a}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =4+\\pi -\\frac{3}{2}-5 \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "2\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\pi -\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi -\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/5d6f61767434332bbb92574ff87d72bda4b07028d77bda5925f73b32a7c751d5.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(2,-2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+lw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2\\pi -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2-2\\pi -8 \\\\  & =-2\\pi -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi -10"] ["math" "-2\\pi -8"] ["math" "-\\pi -12"] ["math" "2\\pi +8"] ["math" "\\pi +12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-49205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\pi -8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/1a4c5c11a2b8ce9c52703ce6c843b3bac16b0527be8affbc974752f12024bb5e.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments, an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(-4,0)$  with radius $3$, and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(1,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-7}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-7,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [1,3]$, and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-7}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-7,-1].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+2+\\pi -2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/state.json" "file_sha256" "dec46a8e884f32f913a276514525581b571e6a2630574d90a614baa5a5eade46"} {"question" "q-14788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2465c6327d30172bcdd0d9d017f382c675629288ef072e8b0ce950d5b9210150"} {"question